---
# ── Minimal Usage ───────────────────────────────────────────
# This file displays "all" configurable items, but you do not have to use them all.
# Just want to write preferences? Delete this entire front matter block (content between ---)
# and write the markdown body directly below (e.g. # Characters / # Style ...).
# Zero formatting, zero YAML required; the editor will review them semantically.
# The structured fields below are the "optional advanced layer"—only used when strict
# deterministic checks like "word count / forbidden words" are needed.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Genre tag: currently passed through as a string to working_memory.user_rules.structured.genre,
# for LLM reference during preference semantic judgment. The genre rules database (assets/rules/genres/)
# is not yet open; the two-stage loader will be restored in the future when built-in files need to be loaded by genre.
genre: xianxia

# Chapter word count range (min-max). Deviations < 20% trigger warnings; ≥ 20% trigger errors.
chapter_words: 3000-6000

# Character/symbol blacklist. Any occurrence (≥ 1 time) triggers an error.
# Note: The Chinese double dash is two em-dash characters; write "——" to match the double em-dash, and "—" for a single em-dash.
forbidden_chars:
  - "——"

# Phrase blacklist. Any occurrence (≥ 1 time) triggers an error. The checker performs literal substring matching without wildcards:
# Only fill in "fixed-length literal strings"; patterns with variables (such as "not X but Y") cannot be caught via literal matching,
# and should be written in the markdown body below for semantic judgment by the editor.
forbidden_phrases:
  - "to some extent"
  - "it is worth noting"

# Fatigue word threshold: warnings are triggered only when the word appears more than N times in this chapter (does not accumulate across chapters).
# Also supports a list format: - word1 - word2 ← default threshold is 1
fatigue_words:
  cannot help but: 1
  unexpectedly: 1
  as if: 2
---

# Style

- Do not use em-dashes instead of commas; dialogues being interrupted are exceptions.
- Avoid analytical report style expressions (e.g., "The core conflict lies in..."), use character actions and dialogues instead.
- Use physical perceptions (e.g., stomach spasm, white knuckles) as much as possible instead of emotional labels (e.g., nervous, angry).

# Characters

- Protagonist "Lin Chen": Calm and restrained, cold outside but warm inside; do not write him as tsundere or overly saintly.
- Supporting character "Junior Sister": Has unspoken but restrained affection for the protagonist; speaks concisely, not talkative.

# Settings

- Cultivation realms: Qi Condensation → Foundation Establishment → Golden Core → Nascent Soul → Soul Transformation, nine layers for each realm.
- Spiritual roots are divided into five elements; the protagonist has a Chaos Spiritual Root (extremely rare).

# Semantic Prompts for Preference Body

Preferences in the Markdown body are semantically evaluated by the editor during review, without strictness level fields:

- **Strictly forbid / Not allowed / Never** — the editor tends to classify violations as critical, triggering rewrites.
- **Avoid / Do not** — the editor tends to classify as error/warning.
- **As much as possible / Ideally** — the editor treats these only as reference preferences, not mandatory.

Mechanical rules (front matter) are strictly checked by `commit_chapter` and return `rule_violations`;
Natural language rules (this Markdown section) are judged by the editor LLM and mapped to the existing seven-dimensional review.

# Instructions for Use

Copy this file into the project-level rules directory `./.ainovel/rules/` to apply (this directory mirrors the global `~/.ainovel/rules/`, and also accepts any `.md` files):

```bash
mkdir -p .ainovel/rules
cp rules.md.example .ainovel/rules/my-rules.md
# Edit .ainovel/rules/my-rules.md to change to your preferences
```

Three layers of loading priority (high → low):

1. Any `.md` in the `./.ainovel/rules/` directory — **Book-specific rules** (bound to the current working directory cwd, merged alphabetically by filename)
2. Any `.md` in the `~/.ainovel/rules/` directory — **Global preferences** (reusable across books, merged alphabetically by filename)
3. `assets/rules/default.md` — **Built-in defaults** (contains the mechanical baseline for AI-tone removal; the semantic baseline is located in the built-in `assets/references/anti-ai-tone.md`)

When multiple sources declare the same field with different values, the **nearest priority** applies; `fatigue_words` are merged by word, and if the same word has different thresholds in multiple sources, the nearest priority is chosen. Conflicts are written to internal diagnostic info (not in LLM context, displayed on demand by the CLI startup panel) without blocking loading.

> AI-tone removal is an example of this mechanism: the built-in baseline (mechanical `default.md` + semantic `anti-ai-tone.md`) acts as a fallback, and your rule `.md` overlays on top of it—write semantic preferences in plain language in the body (main path), and optionally add a front matter section when strict forbidden words/word count checks are needed. Zero compilation required to take effect.

## Running Multiple Books in Parallel

`./.ainovel/rules/` is bound to the **current working directory (Cwd)**, not the novel output directory:

- ✅ Recommended: `cd` to an independent directory for each book and run (`cd works/xiyou && ainovel-cli` / `cd works/honglou && ainovel-cli`) — this isolates `./.ainovel/rules/` for each book.
- ⚠️ Edge case: Switching books using `-o output/xiyou` / `-o output/honglou` from the same cwd — these two books will share the same `./.ainovel/rules/`.

If you need "strict isolation per book" without changing cwd, place your preferences in the global directory `~/.ainovel/rules/` so all books share a single set of user preferences.
